This is a testing file for an API server to stream EMG data on an OpenBCI Ganglion

On the web server, click Start EMG to begin streaming real-time data
Data will appear in the terminal on the host server

To use: 
- run uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000 in terminal
- type in your web browser: <IPv4 address>:8000

Note: This code is adapted for Linux OS/RaspberryPi, in order to use on Windows:
- winget install --interactive usbipd-win (install usbipd tool; in PowerShell as Admin)
- usbipd wsl list (look for OpenBCI dongle -> get BUSID)
- usbipd wsl attach --busid <your-bus-id> (attach dongle to WSL)
- ls /dev/tty* (check if device is visible on Ubuntu (WSL) terminal)
    - the name will either be /dev/ttyACM0 or /dev/ttyUSB0
- sudo adduser $USER dialout (give permission to access serial port (reopen Ubuntu terminal))

Overall Architecture:
[ OpenBCI Ganglion ]
       |
       v
[ Python Server (main.py) ]  <-- Catches the raw data
       |
       +-----------------------------+
       |                             |
  (Path 1: Visualization)       (Path 2: Data Processing)
       |                             |                 |
  [ WebSockets ]                 [ LSL Outlet ]   [ CSV Files ]
       |                             |                 |
       v                             v                 v
  [ Browser ]                  [ Local Network ] 
(You see live EMG data)              
                              

Data Threads:
    1. Main asynchronous thread (FastAPI/Uvicorn)
        - central engine of app, controlled by asyncio Event Loop
        - non-blocking: handles many network requests
        - related functions: manager.connect(), broadcast(), get_running_loop(), gather()
    2. EMG worker thread 
        - handles hardware interaction and data processing
        - blocking: waits for hardware data
        - emg_thread is the actual thread, stop_event is the event flag
        - related functions: 
            - asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(coroutine, loop): how thread 2 communicated with thread 1 (queues the broadcast coroutine onto the Event Loop's schedule)